PostgreSQL (11+). Expand/contract pattern: add columns, backfill, sync trigger. Drop `full_name` later in separate migration once app reads new columns.

## Migration up: `20261124_split_full_name_up.sql`

```sql
-- Run with: psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f 20261124_split_full_name_up.sql
-- Safe to re-run: every statement is idempotent.

-- 1. Add columns (nullable, no default → metadata-only, no table rewrite)
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS first_name text;
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS last_name  text;

-- 2. Split helpers (single source of truth for backfill + trigger)
--    "  Mary  Ann   Smith " -> first='Mary', last='Ann Smith'
--    "Cher"                 -> first='Cher', last=NULL
--    "" / whitespace        -> first='',     last=NULL
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION users_split_first(full text)
RETURNS text LANGUAGE sql IMMUTABLE AS $$
  SELECT coalesce(
           substring(btrim(regexp_replace(full, '\s+', ' ', 'g')) FROM '^(\S+)'),
           '')
$$;

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION users_split_last(full text)
RETURNS text LANGUAGE sql IMMUTABLE AS $$
  SELECT substring(btrim(regexp_replace(full, '\s+', ' ', 'g')) FROM '^\S+ (.+)$')
$$;

-- 3. Sync trigger: keeps old + new columns consistent while both app
--    versions are live (old code writes full_name, new code writes first/last).
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION users_sync_names()
RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
  IF TG_OP = 'INSERT' THEN
    IF NEW.full_name IS NOT NULL AND NEW.first_name IS NULL THEN
      NEW.first_name := users_split_first(NEW.full_name);
      NEW.last_name  := users_split_last(NEW.full_name);
    ELSIF NEW.full_name IS NULL AND NEW.first_name IS NOT NULL THEN
      NEW.full_name := btrim(concat_ws(' ', NEW.first_name, NEW.last_name));
    END IF;
  ELSE -- UPDATE
    IF NEW.full_name IS DISTINCT FROM OLD.full_name THEN
      NEW.first_name := users_split_first(NEW.full_name);
      NEW.last_name  := users_split_last(NEW.full_name);
    ELSIF NEW.first_name IS DISTINCT FROM OLD.first_name
       OR NEW.last_name  IS DISTINCT FROM OLD.last_name THEN
      NEW.full_name := btrim(concat_ws(' ', NEW.first_name, NEW.last_name));
    END IF;
  END IF;
  RETURN NEW;
END $$;

DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS users_sync_names_trg ON users;
CREATE TRIGGER users_sync_names_trg
  BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE OF full_name, first_name, last_name ON users
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION users_sync_names();

-- 4. Batched backfill. Commits per batch → short locks, resumable if killed.
--    Predicate `first_name IS NULL` = "not yet processed"; helper never
--    returns NULL for non-NULL input, so loop always terminates.
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE users_backfill_names(batch_size int DEFAULT 5000)
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  n int;
BEGIN
  LOOP
    WITH batch AS (
      SELECT id FROM users
      WHERE first_name IS NULL AND full_name IS NOT NULL
      ORDER BY id
      LIMIT batch_size
      FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
    )
    UPDATE users u
       SET first_name = users_split_first(u.full_name),
           last_name  = users_split_last(u.full_name)
      FROM batch
     WHERE u.id = batch.id;

    GET DIAGNOSTICS n = ROW_COUNT;
    EXIT WHEN n = 0;
    RAISE NOTICE 'backfilled % rows', n;
    COMMIT;
  END LOOP;
END $$;

CALL users_backfill_names();

-- 5. Verify (should return 0)
SELECT count(*) AS unprocessed
FROM users
WHERE full_name IS NOT NULL AND first_name IS NULL;
```

Note: `CALL` with `COMMIT` inside must run outside explicit transaction block. If migration tool wraps file in `BEGIN/COMMIT` (Flyway, Rails, Alembic default), put step 4's `CALL` in non-transactional migration, or drop the `COMMIT` line and accept one big transaction on small tables.

## Contract (later, separate deploy): `20261208_drop_full_name.sql`

Run only after all app code reads/writes `first_name`/`last_name`.

```sql
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS users_sync_names_trg ON users;
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS users_sync_names();
DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS users_backfill_names(int);

-- Optional constraint; NOT VALID + VALIDATE avoids long exclusive lock
DO $$
BEGIN
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_constraint
                 WHERE conname = 'users_first_name_not_null') THEN
    ALTER TABLE users ADD CONSTRAINT users_first_name_not_null
      CHECK (first_name IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;
  END IF;
END $$;
ALTER TABLE users VALIDATE CONSTRAINT users_first_name_not_null;

ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS full_name;  -- irreversible: data loss
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS users_split_first(text);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS users_split_last(text);
```

## Down (reverses up only, before contract)

```sql
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS users_sync_names_trg ON users;
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS users_sync_names();
DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS users_backfill_names(int);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS users_split_first(text);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS users_split_last(text);
ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS first_name;
ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS last_name;
```

## Why safe re-run

- `IF NOT EXISTS` / `CREATE OR REPLACE` / `DROP ... IF EXISTS` → DDL idempotent.
- Backfill filter `first_name IS NULL` → already-done rows skipped; crash mid-run resumes where left.
- Trigger covers writes during backfill → no rows missed by race.
- `SKIP LOCKED` → no blocking against live traffic.

## Caveats

- Name splitting lossy by nature: "Ludwig van Beethoven" → first `Ludwig`, last `van Beethoven`; mononyms, family-name-first cultures, honorifics not handled. Consider keeping `full_name` (or a `display_name`) permanently instead of dropping.
- Large table: add temp partial index `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ... ON users(id) WHERE first_name IS NULL` to speed batch scans; drop after.
- Assumes `id` primary key. Adjust if different.
